.SH DISPLAY STRING

.PP
The \(aqdisplay string\(aq is either passed on the command-line, or set using the \(aqdisplay\(aq or \(aqdisplay-string\(aq options in the config file. It specifies the string to be displayed in the bar. The \(aqdisplay string\(aq  can contain variables that are substituted with values gathered from the system, and formatting strings that effect colors, positioning and clickable areas. Variables have the form 
.na 
\fB$(name)\fR and formatting strings start with 
.na 
\fB~\fR (tilde).
.PP
The variable values come in two types, plain, and auto-colored. Auto-colored values change color depending on the level of the value.
.PP
And example display string might look like:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	$(time) host: $(hostname) rootfs: $(fs:/) today: $(date)
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
As the \(aq$\(aq symbol means something to the shell, the display string will have to be supplied within single quotes if passed on the command line. Alternatively the format \(aq^(\(aq can be used instead of \(aq$(\(aq, like so:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	^(time) ^(hostname) ^(fs:/) ^(date)
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Colors and other formatting within the display string can be set using libUseful 
.na 
\fB~\fR notation, where the next character is the color prefix. Available colors are:
.PP
.in +2

.TP
.B w
white foreground

.TP
.B n
black foreground

.TP
.B b
blue foreground

.TP
.B c
cyan foreground

.TP
.B g
green foreground

.TP
.B y
yellow foreground

.TP
.B m
magenta foreground

.TP
.B r
red foreground

.TP
.B W
white background

.TP
.B N
black background

.TP
.B B
blue background

.TP
.B C
cyan background

.TP
.B G
green background

.TP
.B Y
yellow background

.TP
.B M
magenta background

.TP
.B R
red background
.in

.PP
\*(ti>{pixels} :position following text \(aqpixels\(aq into the bar (currently only with dzen2 bars)  display image file at path (default xpm, but can display jpeg and png images provided imagemagik \(aqconvert\(aq is installed) define a clickable area reset to default color  unicode code point unicode glyph name
.PP
So, for example:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	~bdisk:~0 $(fs:/)  ~bmem:~0 $(mem)
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Will display the words \(aqdisk:\(aq and \(aqmem:\(aq in blue, in front of the values for root-partition usage and memory usage, which will be in the default color.
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	~R~w$(hour):$(minutes)\*(ti0 $(date)
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Will display the time (hours and minutes) in white on a red background, and the date with the default colors.
.PP
Dzen2 supports images, and these can be set with the notation 
.na 
\fB~i{/usr/share/icons/world.jpg}\fR. Dzen2 only supports .xpm images, so barmaid.lua will use the ImageMagick \(aqconvert\(aq utility to convert .png or .jpg 
.PP
Some values have alternative versions that are suffixed with \(aq:color\(aq. These values are automatically colored according to their numeric values. Values can also be modified for display using the \(aqreformat module\(aq method discussed in the \(aqMODULES\(aq section below.
.PP
