.in

.PP
Please note, any value that has \(aq:\(aq at the end, takes an argument, like 
.na 
\fBbat:1\fR or 
.na 
\fBip4address:eth0\fR.
.PP
\(aqfreemem\(aq and \(aqavailmem\(aq, \(aqfree\(aq and \(aqavail\(aq, and \(aqmem\(aq and \(aqmemuse\(aq differ. \(aqfreemem\(aq, \(aqfree\(aq and \(aqmem\(aq are calcluated to align with the output of the command-line \(aqfree\(aq command. \(aqavailmem\(aq, \(aqavail\(aq and \(aqmemuse\(aq are calculated from the /proc/meminfo \(aqMemAvailable\(aq entry. Usually there should be little difference between these, but one cause of a difference is ramdisks. If you have a tmpfs ramdisk on, say /tmp, and its consuming a lot of memory (perhaps because it contains large files) \(aqfreemem\(aq and \(aqmem\(aq will show you have plenty of memory, even though you don\(aqt, as they will not be aware of memory consumed by the ramdisk. \(aqavailmem\(aq and \(aqmemuse\(aq will be a truer reflection of memory available. If you display both these values, and see a large difference between them, then perhaps you need to check your ramdisks!
.PP
The \(aqip4\(aq values have a special case where the interface is specified as \(aqdefault\(aq e.g. \(aqip4address:default\(aq. In this case details are returned for the first interface that isn\(aqt the local interface and has an ip address. 
.SH TERMINAL BOTTOM BAR

.PP
If barmaid is run in a terminal with the 
.na 
\fB\-y\fR argument set to 
.na 
\fBbottom\fR, like so:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	lua barmaid.lua \-t term \-y bottom \-bg blue
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Then a bar will be displayed at the bottom of the terminal, with normal terminal output being limited to the lines above it. This is an experimental feature. It\(aqs been seen to work in xterm and qterminal, though \(aqvi\(aq in xterm seems to have some cosmetic issues.
.SH UNICODE

.PP
Barmaid supports unicode UTF8 output. Unicode symbols can be included by either:
.PP
.in +2
1) Unicode code-point value. so, for example "U266B" displays a musical note symbol.
.br
2) Unicode glyph name. This requires a version of libUseful more recent than 4.38 and an '/etc/unicode-names.conf' file. This allows specifying unicode symbols via the notation: ":music:"
.br
.in

.SH VALUE TRANSLATION

.PP
There are a two ways to translate a datavalue into something else for display. For instance, some datavalues hold the string \(aqup\(aq or \(aqdown\(aq to indictate the state of something. Reformat modules are lua plug-ins used to perform this task and are discussed in the \(aqMODULES\(aq section below. The other method for translating such values is the \(aq-tr\(aq command-line option, or the \(aqtranslate\(aq config-file option. In both cases this system uses a configuration string of the form:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  <value>|<translation>
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
So for example, the following:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  up|~g up ~0
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Could be used to color the string \(aqup\(aq in green (for clarity extra spaces are added around \(aqup\(aq in the translation). This method could also be used to map \(aqup\(aq to an icon:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  up|\*(tii{/usr/share/icons/okay.jpg}
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
This would map all values that consist of the word \(aqup\(aq to the specified icon.
.PP
Sometimes there\(aqs a need to specify which value is being translated. Multiple different data lookups could return the same value, and you might want to color them differently. This is achieved with:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  <name>=<value>|<translation>
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Where \(aqname\(aq is the name of a value, and \(aqvalue\(aq is it\(aqs actual displayed result. E.g.
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  up:google.com:80=up|~gG~0
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Could be used to supply a green \(aqG\(aq to indicate google is accessible, but not interfere with any other values that return \(aqup\(aq.
.PP
The \(aqkey\(aq of the translation (i.e. \(aqname=value\(aq) can contain shell/fnmatch-style wildcards. The symbols 
.na 
\fB*\fR, 
.na 
\fB+\fR, 
.na 
\fB?\fR, 
.na 
\fB[\fR and 
.na 
\fB]\fR will be honored with their shell/fnmatch meanings. 
.na 
\fB\\\fR can be used to quote these characters.
.PP
EXAMPLE:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  barmaid.lua \(aqdns:$(dnsup:google.com)  $(up:google.com:80) $(up:freshcode.club:80) $(up:kernel.org:80)\(aq \-tr \(aqdnsup:google.com=up|~gup~0\(aq \-tr \(aqdnsup:google.com=down|~rDOWN~0\(aq \-tr \(aqup:google.com:80=up|~gG~0\(aq \-tr \(aqup:freshcode.club:80=up|~gF~0\(aq \-tr \(aqup:kernel.org:80=up|~gK~0\(aq
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
This allows mapping the value \(aqup\(aq for different variables to different output strings (admittedly all of them green in color).
.SH KEY-VALUE DATA

.PP
There are two ways that key-value data can be loaded into barmaid and added to the list of values that can be displayed. One method is via \(aqkey value files\(aq. For this method the path to a file is supplied using the 
.na 
\fB\-kvfile\fR command-line argument. Lines the form "<name>=<value>" are then read from this file and added to the list of named values that can be displayed using the `$(name)` notation. The other method is via a unix-filesystem socket. The path to the socket is specified with the '-sock' command-line option, and then values can be written to it in the line-by-line format "<name>=<value>" (so the same as for the key-value files). Both files and the socket method expect a single "<name>=<value>" per line, and expect lines to be terminated with a line-feed character.
.PP
Both `-kvfile` and `-sock` support special name-value pairs whose name begins with '@'. These are treated as counters, so that instead of storing the sent value, the counter increments. If a blank string is sent as the value, the counter will reset to zero. 
.PP
Both `-kvfile` and `-sock` support special name-value pairs whose name begins with '>'. These work like the counters above, but in addition are stored as lists in files at '~/.barmaid/<name>.lst\(aq. An on-click can then be used to launch some program that will display the list.  If a blank string is sent as the value the counter will reset to zero and the list will be cleared. 
.SH ANIMATIONS

.PP
Since barmaid v6.5 simple text animations are supported. They are defined as comma-seperated lists of strings like so:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

\*(tia{1,2,3,4}
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
This animation would count from 1 to 4 over and over. 
.PP
These are normally used in Value Translations, for instance to have an animation that plays when a battery is charging.
.PP
Clever use of selected chars can create text spinners, bubblers, etc
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

\*(tia{\\,|./,\-}                               \- classic \(aqspinner\(aq
\*(tia{.,o,O}                                 \- \(aqbubbler\(aq
\*(tia{|\-\-\-,\-|\-\-,\-\-|\-,\-\-\-|,\-\-|\-,\-|\-\-,|\-e\-\-    \- line that sweeps back and forth
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
However a monospace font is usually needed for such animations to look good.
.PP
Color codes like \(aq\*(tir\(aq can be used in animations:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

~a{~r\-~0\-\-,\-~r\-~0\-,\-\-~r\-~0,\-~r\-\*(ti0\-}       \- \(aqknight rider\(aq red bar that moves back and forth
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.SH MODULES

.PP
Since version 3.0 barmaid supports modules. These are small lua scripts placed in a directory (default path /usr/local/lib/barmaid/:/usr/lib/barmaid:\*(ti/.local/lib/barmaid) that can be used to extend barmaid\(aqs functionality. Two types of module exist: \(aqInformation modules\(aq that add new types of information to be displayed, and \(aqReformat modules\(aq that color, translate or otherwise modify the values to be displayed. 
.PP
Two example information  modules already exist \(aqaurorawatch.lua\(aq and \(aqisc.lua\(aq, displaying the aurorawatch status and Internet Storm Center Threat Level respectively. Modules work by putting an object into the table \(aqlookup_modules\(aq. This object must have a \(aq.init\(aq function. This function will be called and passed a \(aqlookups\(aq table and a \(aqdisplay string\(aq. This string contains all the variables that are needed to display the bar. The module should check if the name of the variable it will provide is in the string, and if so add a \(aqlookup\(aq function to the supplied \(aqlookups\(aq table. This lookup function will then be called when the data is needed. The module should get the data and put it into the table \(aqdisplay_values\(aq under the name that it will be called as. For instance, the Internet Storm Center threat level can be looked up via the variable \(aq$(isc)\(aq and so it\(aqs value is put into the \(aqdisplay_values\(aq table like this:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

	display_values["isc"]=value
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
Some values, particularly those looked up via the internet, should not be looked up on every call of the lookup function. To assist with this there\(aqs a global \(aqlookup_counter\(aq value that counts seconds since program startup. 
.PP
\(aqReformat\(aq modules are modules intended for the use of changing the displayed value that\(aqs output to the bar. Instead of being added to \(aqlookup_modules\(aq table the object is added to the \(aqdisplay_modules\(aq table. The don\(aqt need an \(aqinit\(aq function, and the object aded to the \(aqdisplay_modules\(aq table only needs to contain as single function called \(aqprocess\(aq. This function is called for every lookup value/type, and is passed the variables \(aqname\(aq and \(aqvalue\(aq, representing the name and value of each variable that\(aqs going to be displayed. The function either returns \(aqvalue\(aq unchanged, or else return some kind of translated value.
.PP
An example \(aqreformat\(aq module is also provided in the distribution.
.SH CONFIG FILE

.PP
By default barmaid looks for config files in 
.na 
\fB~/.config/barmaid.lua/barmaid.conf\fR, 
.na 
\fB~/.config/barmaid.conf\fR, 
.na 
\fB~/.barmaid.conf\fR and 
.na 
\fB/etc/barmaid.conf\fR. The \(aq-c\(aq command-line option allows changing this search path, like so:
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  barmaid.lua \-c /config/barmaid.conf:\*(ti/etc/barmaid.conf:/usr/local/etc/barmaid.conf
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
 
.PP
The config file contains entries of the form:
.br
  
.nf
.ft CW
.RS
.EX

  <config type> <value>
.RE
.EE

.fi
.ft P
  Possible config types are:
.PP
.in +2

.TP
.B display
string to be displayed in the bar (multiple can be configured and cycled through)

.TP
.B display-string
string to be displayed in the bar (multiple can be configured and cycled through)

.TP
.B display_cycle_time
when multiple display strings are configured, cycle between them at interval defined in seconds

.TP
.B output
output type, \(aqdzen2\(aq, \(aqlemonbar\(aq, \(aqdwm\(aq, etc

.TP
.B outtype
output type, \(aqdzen2\(aq, \(aqlemonbar\(aq, \(aqdwm\(aq, etc

.TP
.B xpos
x-position, can be \(aqleft\(aq, \(aqright\(aq, \(aqcenter\(aq or a pixel-position

.TP
.B ypos
y-position, can be \(aqleft\(aq, \(aqright\(aq, \(aqcenter\(aq or a pixel-position

.TP
.B width
bar width in pixels

.TP
.B height
bar height in pixels

.TP
.B align
